# Back link

A back link returns to the previous step of a task in the state the user left it.

These are civica.uk reference implementations for component authors. They do not replace native Home Assistant behaviour or demonstrate a connection to a live home.

## When to use it

Use it in a multi-step setup or form when returning to change an answer is useful. Keep the destination predictable and preserve non-secret answers.

## When not to use it

Do not add a back link to every dashboard card, use it alongside breadcrumbs, or treat browser history as a guaranteed parent route. A deep link may have no meaningful in-app previous page.

## Home Assistant adaptation

Home Assistant already owns routing and many back controls. Reuse the native route and editor state rather than inserting a second competing control. The examples use local section targets so no real dashboard is left or modified.

## Accessibility and behaviour

Use a real link when navigating. If returning requires saving a form answer, use the owning form’s submit behaviour rather than disguising a state-changing operation as a link. Position it before main content and after the service header.

